 The executor observes only its exact child tree and binds Linux pidfds to PID/startTicks before any cleanup signal; there is no numeric-PID signaling fallback. It preserves the original native first failure and raw first/secondary method errors, stdout/stderr, own process receipts, build-info dictionaries, original-pixel PNGs/pose JSON and unedited raw WebMs. It stream-hashes evidence using Python integer sizes rather than a 32 MiB buffered read. Kernel-owned closure and localhost refusal are real observations, not a relabelled closedAt timestamp. Forced cleanup or executor/watchdog error cannot restore success. The outer watchdog's extra 120 seconds is only failed-case cleanup tolerance; it cannot extend the original playable budget or turn a timed-out tool into a pass.
 
